{"type":"text","timestamp":1791653807600,"sessionID":"ses_1ef25f504a463010b755","part":{"id":"prt_126e385e9001pp9jnkFb2sQ3pT","sessionID":"ses_1ef25f504a463010b755","messageID":"msg_126e37205001WWBlptp74gzHEm","type":"text","text":"The standard check (`python3 check.py`) now fails on direct calls to `eval` in `src`. The only change is in `checks.json`:\n\n```json\n{\"forbidden_builtins\": [\"eval\"]}\n```\n\n`check.py` didn't need any changes. It reads the code's structure instead of searching the text, and it only flags a call when `eval` is used by its bare name.\n\nI tested it with throwaway files in `src`, then deleted them:\n- The current code still passes (exit 0).\n- A comment containing `eval(x)`, the string `\"eval(1)\"` and `obj.eval(1)` all pass (exit 0).\n- `eval(\"1\")` fails, and the check names the file (exit 1).\n\n**Gaps:** the check doesn't catch indirect uses like `builtins.eval(...)`, `e = eval; e(...)` or `getattr(__builtins__, \"eval\")`. `builtins.eval(...)` looks the same to the check as `obj.eval(...)`, so blocking it would mean changing `check.py` to look at where the method comes from. Tell me if you want that.","time":{"start":1791653807593,"end":1791653807600}}}
